KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ assignment of \{\} value-initializes a scoped enum: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ assignment of \{\} to an int: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [expr.ass]/9: `x = {}' with a braced-init-list means `x = T{}', i.e.
the operand is value-initialized ([dcl.init.list]/3.11) -- for a scoped
enumeration the enumerator with value zero.  The C++ front end rejects the
assignment ("invalid implicit conversion from '<<type:>>' to 'enum errc'").
libstdc++'s <charconv> writes `__res.ec = {};' in __to_chars_10/16/8/2 and
__to_chars, so std::to_chars for every integer type is dropped (audit
residue item 7, round 162; cpp17_from_chars_unsigned, cpp20_format_header).
